  "content": {
    "id": "p18-problem-2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "[Multiple Root Theorem]",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p18-problem-2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Define the polynomial\n$$P(x)=(x-1)^3(1+x^2).$$\nShow that $P(1)=P'(1)=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(1)=P'(1)=0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*} P(1)&=(1-1)^3(1+1^2)=0,\\\\ P'(x)&=3(x-1)^2(1+x^2)+2x(x-1)^3,\\\\ P'(1)&=3(0)^2(2)+2(1)(0)^3=0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p18-problem-2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Define the polynomial\n$$P(x)=(x-\\alpha)^nQ(x),$$\nwhere $Q(x)$ is a polynomial and $n\\ge 2$ is an integer. Show that\n$$P'(x)=(x-\\alpha)^{n-1}\\bigl[nQ(x)+(x-\\alpha)Q'(x)\\bigr],$$\nand hence state the value of $P'(\\alpha)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P'(\\alpha)=0$.\n\nThe product rule leaves the common factor $(x-\\alpha)^{n-1}$, which vanishes at $x=\\alpha$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*} P'(x)&=n(x-\\alpha)^{n-1}Q(x)+(x-\\alpha)^nQ'(x)\\\\ P'(x)&=(x-\\alpha)^{n-1}\\bigl[nQ(x)+(x-\\alpha)Q'(x)\\bigr].\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $n\\ge 2$, the exponent $n-1$ is positive. The factor $(x-\\alpha)^{n-1}$ is zero at $x=\\alpha$, so $P'(\\alpha)=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
